Description of AX.25 'C' code  -- Phil Karn, KA9Q     October 21, 1984
					Updated:      June 9, 1985

Overview

The code currently implements AX.25 Level 2 as defined in the Version 2.0
document published by the ARRL. Depending on the choice of low level driver
routines, it will use either the Zilog SIO (native to the 820, but requiring
an external receive clock recovery "state machine" circuit) or the
Zilog 8530 (requiring the FADCA daughter board, or "FAD board") for HDLC
communications. In both cases, an external modem between the SIO or 8530
and the radio is needed.

This code contains a number of extensions to AX.25 which are new with
Version 2.0:

1. Idle link keep-alive polls (timer T3)
2. Acknowledgement delay (timer T1)
3. "C" bits and correct P/F bit usage according to LAPB

Item 3 is upward compatible in that it is not used whenever the software
detects an "old" (i.e., pre-Version 2.0) implementation of AX.25 (which
at this point includes all other AX.25 implementations.)  The keep-alive
polls require the proper use of the P/F bit and will not be sent to
an old implementation.

This code also supports multiple simultaneous logical links. This is an
implementation rather than a protocol issue; it involves keeping individual
"link descriptors" for each station, and using a hash lookup algorithm to
find the appropriate link descriptor whenever a frame is received. Support is
also provided for multiple physical HDLC ports so that more than one channel
can be used.

These extensions required me to separate and classify the commands and
parameters (from a unit like the TAPR TNC) into groups that are applied on a
per-link, -line or -terminal basis as appropriate.

Digipeating works, but currently it will repeat a frame only on the physical
link on which it was received. With relatively little work it would be
possible to do cross-channel digipeating, although with the work underway to
define and implement higher level protocols it is not clear this is a "clean"
thing to do.  Hooks are provided for true level-3 packet switching, e.g.,
with the ARPA Internet Protocol (IP), assuming there is memory left.

A number of things which are in the TAPR TNC are not here. Some of
these I haven't gotten around to implementing yet. Others, such as
CW identification and the Vancouver protocol are obsolete in my opinion
and I don't really feel like putting them in.

Software description

General

All I/O is interrupt driven. Full use is made of the 820's interrupt
capability; queues are emptied or filled with characters going to
and from the terminal and SIO or 8530.  A top-level loop in main.c executes
higher level functions which operate on the "other ends" of the interrupt
queues.  The CTC timer ticks at a 10 hertz rate and provides all real-time
functions.  A halt instruction at the end of the main loop causes the CPU
to wait until an interrupt occurs; this might help reduce RFI and/or
power consumption on a CMOS version of the Z-80.  It also makes possible
the use of a "CPU speedometer" which monitors the HALT line from the CPU
and indicates the amount of real time being used by the software.

ax25.h

The master file of global data structures, defined constants, etc. Included
by just about every .c file, although it should probably be broken
up into more manageable pieces.

820.h, 820.c, line.c, inout.asm, 820ints.asm, fad.h, fad.c

These files hopefully contain all of the machine-dependent code.
820.h, 820.c and inout.asm contains the usual stuff to do bit and port
level twiddling on the Z-80 family chips and the 820 machine.

820ints.asm sets up the register save and restore sequences necessary to call
a C routine directly from an interrupt.

line.c implements routines to read and write raw HDLC frames which, despite
my best efforts at portability, are tied closely to the way the Zilog SIO
operates. It would probably have to be rewritten for a different HDLC chip.

If you are using a FADCA daughter board (with the 8530 add-on), the two files
fad.h and fad.c replace the three files 820.h, 820.c and line.c.

queue.c

Provides primitives getframe() and putframe() which operate on linked-list
queues of an important internal structure called, naturally enough, a "frame".
Two other primitives, allocframe() and freeframe() dynamically create and
destroy frame structures from a common storage heap.

"Fifos" containing individual characters are also provided for terminal
I/O; putf() and getf() operate on these.

addr.c, hash.c

Provides those addressing functions which are unique to AX.25.
It was my intention here to segregate our changes to X.25 to these
files as much as possible.  The function "addrproc" examines the
address fields in the raw frames received from the channel; it also performs
digipeating (if enabled).  It passes frames which the link level protocol
should see (addressed to us and passed all digipeaters) up to the next level
with a command/response indication (if any) derived from the C bits
in the address fields. It uses the hash algorithms in hash.c to find the
appropriate link table entry for the frame, creating a new entry if needed.

sendframe() provides a complementary service for the transmitter.
It takes the link table pointer along with the various components of the
frame (command/response indication, control and data fields), builds
up a frame and queues it on the appropriate HDLC line queue.

link.c

This is the heart of the Level 2 protocol. process() implements all state
transitions and side effects (e.g., timer control) which are required each
time a frame is received. Most of the function consists of nested switch
statements, corresponding to the combinations of received frame type and
current state which dictate each action. This implementation style (as
opposed to a state table) was a matter of personal choice. I found switch
statements easier to read and easier to implement special side effects
(such as changing state variables). I am not that adverse to using a state
table version, but this one seems to work...

After a frame has been processed, the response, if any, is sent to
the transmitter.  Properly received I-frames are passed up to the
next level.

ackours(), frmr(), setstate() and sendctl() are subsidiaries to process();
they are pulled out mainly to keep process() from becoming excessively large
(it still is.) ltxstart() is a "kick" or "priming" routine that tries to
move transmit data through the system whenever possible. It is called from a
number of places whenever it might have just become possible to do this
(e.g., when an acknowledgement has been received) but of course, it frequently
can do nothing. This overhead is an unhappy artifact of the lack of a "real"
operating system base that would support processes and their associated
sleep/wakeup facilities.

timer.c

This file contains functions which implement real-time timers using a
continuous 10 Hz clock tick.  Each timer has three states: stopped,
running and expired. A timer is a data structure which is put on a 
doubly-linked list whenever it is running, and automatically removed
when it is stopped or expires. This allows the clock-tick routine
(which could gobble up a fair bit of time since it runs frequently) to
be as simple and fast as possible. User routines must poll the state
of the timer to see if it has expired; there is no direct automatic
execution of a function upon expiration. timer_srv(), called from the
main loop, does this by calling various other routines in the file as
appropriate to recover from link timeouts, etc.  Note that the only thing
done by the clock interrupt handler proper is the setting of a flag which
the higher level routines act on; this is to prevent a lot of computation
from occurring at interrupt time, possibly starving other interrupts.

term.c

Handles all terminal related processing, e.g, input line editing and received
I-frame output processing.  A number of low-level utility functions
for terminal output via a common fifo (e.g., put string and format
functions) are also provided.  Output processing uses an interrupt-driven
queue; while this isn't needed on the 820 because of the memory-mapped
display (in fact it slows it down a bit), writing it in this manner
provides for output flow control and also allows the code to be easily
modified to support a terminal on a UART port, such as the B channel of the
SIO if it isn't needed for HDLC.

trace.c

Handles the display of frames. There are three display modes, encoded into
the "tdata" argument of dump(): 0, meaning no display; 1, providing an
abbreviated display containing the address and control fields broken out
into "human-readable" form, and 2, which in addition provides a hex, ascii
and shifted-ascii raw dump of the frame.

control.c

Top level control functions for level 2.  These include link connect and
disconnect, as well as interfaces for higher level code to write data buffers
to a link.  A main-loop function, level2(), is there to move data on queues
in and out of the level two code.  The hook for level 3, protosw(), is also
here; this is where protocol demultiplexing for I-frames belonging a given
level 3 protocol e.g., DoD IP (or even, God forbid, X.25 level 3...gak!)
will be done. The current level 3 "protocol", hex F0, is interpreted to mean
"send directly to the terminal".

command.h, command.c, cmdtab.c, cmdrout.c

These files implement the command parser. This is a surprisingly large
section of the whole code, reflecting all of the data structures which may
be examined and changed by the user. command.c contains general common
routines to parse command lines and search the tables, while cmdtab.c
contains the tables to bind command strings to function names. cmdrout.c
contains (most of) the functions which implement these user commands.

misc.c

misc.c contains a number of fairly low level utility routines which do such
things as convert ascii strings to AX.25 shifted-ascii format and back,
compare addresses, classify control fields, construct digipeater reply paths,
etc.

init.c

Various hardware and data structure initialization routines, collected
into one place.

space.c

space.c is where space for the external data data structures is defined.
Some C compilers complain bitterly if you have global definitions of the
form "int x;" in more than one file. My solution is to make the include
file contain definitions of the form "EXTERN int x;". In all .c files except
for space.c, EXTERN is defined as "extern"; in space.c, the "EXTERN" prefix
is dropped. This is all done with a little preprocessor magic.  A bit kludgey,
perhaps, but it is a technique now widely used by C programmers, and it DOES
allow you to keep all your definitions in one place.

mkconf.c

This is a standalone program (not linked with the TNC code) which generates
a PARAM.PAD file with some fairly reasonable defaults. There is no need to
modify these defaults because you can always load up the PARAM.PAD file with
the 'load' command, change them and 'save' them back to disk. mkconf just
allows you to get started.

Summary

This is a necessarily sketchy outline of what I've done here. Hopefully,
I have done enough of a good job commenting the code that along with this
overview you shouldn't have too much trouble understanding what's going
on.

Naturally, like any good software hacker, I'm hard at work making improvements
and additions to this code, so things are subject to change. In particular,
I have the following planned:

1. Adding DoD Internet Protocol as a Layer 3 module, along with the ARP
(address resolution protocol).

2. Improved support for serial terminals. There should be a command to
switch the console over to the B channel of the SIO; better yet, the
code should support multiple terminals in the way it now supports
multiple HDLC channels, but there's only so much space on a Z80.

3. Speed improvements. I'm rather disappointed with the speed of this code
on the 820, although a 2.5 Mhz Z-80 isn't the fastest machine in the world.
Channel speed is completely dominated by the interrupt routines in line.c,
and while I've made them as tight as I can, some improvement might be had
by coding sections of them in assembler.  However, I'd MUCH rather have DMA
hardware available, as this is the only way to get the really dramatic
speedup we need to run speeds like 56 kb and up.

4. Improved deadlock protection. There is a lot of dynamic memory allocation
going on, and although I throttle links with RNRs when necessary, there are
still other ways that deadlock might occur, e.g., by having a very large
number of entries in the link table (there is currently no enforced limit.)

I hope this first release of my code is useful to you. Just because I'm
continuing to work on it doesn't keep you from also doing so - I would dearly
love to get back in return any enhancements or bug fixes. I'd also like
comments on the program itself: does it solve your needs? What design flaws
really bother you? Are the commands too awkward? Etc. Remember though that
this is a labor of love for me, and I certainly won't be able to please
everybody.  
